narrative:
## Mathematical Set Operations

Set supports standard set theory operations: union, intersection, and difference.

### Union — All Elements

```xiom
let a = Set[Int]();
a.insert(1); a.insert(2); a.insert(3);

let b = Set[Int]();
b.insert(3); b.insert(4); b.insert(5);

let union = a.union(&b);  // {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
```

Union combines elements from both sets — duplicates are automatically removed.

### Intersection — Common Elements

```xiom
let a = Set[Int]();
a.insert(1); a.insert(2); a.insert(3);

let b = Set[Int]();
b.insert(2); b.insert(3); b.insert(4);

let common = a.intersection(&b);  // {2, 3}
```

Intersection returns elements present in **both** sets.

### Difference — Elements in A but not B

```xiom
let a = Set[Int]();
a.insert(1); a.insert(2); a.insert(3);

let b = Set[Int]();
b.insert(3); b.insert(4);

let diff = a.difference(&b);  // {1, 2}
```

Difference returns elements in the **first** set that are **not** in the second.

### Practical Examples

```xiom
// Find shared interests between users
fn shared_interests(user_a: &Set[Str], user_b: &Set[Str]) -> Set[Str] {
  return user_a.intersection(user_b);
}

// Find which permissions a user is missing
fn missing_permissions(have: &Set[Str], needed: &Set[Str]) -> Set[Str] {
  return needed.difference(have);
}

// Combine privileges from multiple roles
fn all_permissions(roles: &Vec[Set[Str]]) -> Set[Str] {
  let result = Set[Str]();
  var i = 0;
  while i < roles.len() {
    result = result.union(&roles[i]);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Is Subset / Is Superset

```xiom
let a = Set[Int](); a.insert(1); a.insert(2);
let b = Set[Int](); b.insert(1); b.insert(2); b.insert(3);

// a is a subset of b
let is_sub = a.is_subset(&b);  // true
// b is a superset of a
let is_super = b.is_superset(&a);  // true
```

